/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Write a continuous-time system as an operator T: the input x(t) goes in and the output y(t)=T{x(t)} comes out, with t in seconds.
   - x₁(t) and x₂(t) are any two inputs, a and b are any constants, and y₁=T{x₁}, y₂=T{x₂} are their outputs.
   - Put a=b=0 and the rule says T{0}=0: a linear system gives zero output for zero input.
   - t₀ is any delay in seconds (positive t₀ delays, negative t₀ advances).
   - A system that obeys both rules is LTI.
   - Each test compares two paths through the system.
   - Three patterns settle most exam systems.
   - Any input can be written as a continuous sum, that is an integral, of shifted impulses: x(t)=∫ x(τ)δ(t−τ)dτ, taken over all τ.
   - So one measured signal, h(t), predicts the response to every input.
*/
