/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - δ(t) is zero for every t ≠ 0 and has unit area.
   - h(t) belongs to the system, not to any input.
   - For an RC low-pass filter (output across C) with time constant τ=RC, h(t)=(1/τ)e−t/τu(t).
   - A unit step is the running integral of the unit impulse: u(t)=∫−∞t δ(λ)dλ, with λ a dummy variable.
   - s(t) is the step response, h(λ) is the impulse response at time λ, and λ is a dummy variable of integration.
   - the impulse response is the slope of the step response.
   - For the RC filter, s(t)=∫0t(1/τ)e−λ/τdλ=(1−e−t/τ)u(t).
   - Figure 2.2.1 plots h(t) and s(t) for the RC filter of the worked example (τ=1 ms).
   - On a bench, a narrow pulse stands in for δ(t) if its width is much shorter than τ, and a square wave with a long period gives repeated steps.
*/
