/* book exam traps: Swapping the relations: s(t) is the integral of h(t) and h(t) is the derivative of s(t), never the other way round. | Dropping u(t): the RC filter has h(t)=0 and s(t)=0 for t<0. Writing h(t)=1000e−1000t with no u(t) claims a huge output before the impulse arrives. | Losing the 1/τ in h(t): for R=1 kΩ and C=1 μF, h(0+)=1000 s−1, not 1. | Sign of the derivative: d/dt(1−e−t/τ)=+(1/τ)e−t/τ. The minus from the exponent cancels the minus in front. A negative h for a charging RC circuit is a sign slip. | Missing the impulse when s(t) jumps: s(t)=(1−0.5e−2t)u(t) jumps by 0.5 at t=0, so h(t) contains 0.5δ(t). */
